WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:06.430
The Purge has evolved from a low-budget home
invasion horror into a politically charged

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:11.660
survival franchise over the course of its
three installments so far, but before this

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.619
vision of a dystopian hellscape began to have
such scary similarities to current events,

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:23.689
there were some real-life tragedies that inspired
the movie -- and some scary events that followed

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:27.709
the films as well to make it a full circle
experience.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.800
"Thank you for your sacrifice."

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.440
Here's the untold truth of The Purge.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.440
Wicked history

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.781
The first question on most people's minds
after watching a Purge movie is "what would

00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:42.810
happen if they really did that?"

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.320
And the answer is, well, they kinda did.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.620
Roman Pagans used to celebrate a festival
called Saturnalia, which was a week-long period

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:59.230
of lawlessness that allowed citizens to go
crazy and get out all their pent-up aggressions

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.999
without fear of retribution right before Christmas.

00:01:03.999 --> 00:01:11.820
Of course, there were winners and losers in
this open season approach to crime, and the

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:18.170
whole purpose was to show the masses what
life would be like without the rule of law,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.450
but still.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.430
Those Romans were crazy.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.280
The aftermath

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:30.190
Unfortunately the real-life parallels between
The Purge and society don't stop there.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:36.909
In May 2016, an Indiana teen was arrested
for kidnapping a woman at a fast food restaurant,

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:43.170
and he was then connected to a string of murders
over a three-day spree that he referred to

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.180
as a nightly purge.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.520
"Mr. Cruise went on a four day one man purge."

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.289
Futile resistance

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:59.079
When writer-director James DeMonaco first
came up with the idea for The Purge, he decided

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:04.430
to run it by his dad, who just so happened
to remember something similar happening on

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:08.030
a Star Trek episode with a similar premise.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:13.440
In the original series, Kirk and Spock are
trying to help a planet whose people are dictated

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.730
by an artificially intelligent authoritarian
computer system, and they're shocked to find

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:25.190
out that every day at 6 p.m., "the Red Hour,"
the computer shuts down and people are free

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.660
to go nuts with all of their wicked ways.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.160
It's situations like this when that old phrase
comes in handy…

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:34.480
"Beam me aboard."

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.239
Personal rage

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:41.680
When you consider the high levels of bloodshed
needed to bring his dark vision for America

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.280
to the big screen, it might seem odd that
DeMonaco has a fear of firearms, but the gun

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.920
violence that seems to grip the US every other
week was actually one of the reasons he wanted

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.850
to make the Purge films.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.840
The Brooklyn-born filmmaker found himself
gripped by the news coverage every time a

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:07.080
story about a shooting was on, finally coming
to the realization that traditional villains

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.140
have become far less scary than the random
man on the street.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.450
His firearm phobia wouldn't begin to develop
into an idea for a feature film until he found

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.770
himself in a violent road rage incident.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:26.160
DeMonaco revealed that he once became involved
in a fight with another motorist after the

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.960
driver in question almost killed the director
and his wife while drunk behind the wheel.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.530
DeMonaco reportedly wrestled the man to the
ground while police were on their way, and

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:43.490
something his wife said after the fracas became
the root of the idea behind Purging as a concept.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:44.490
DeMonaco recalled:

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.900
"This guy had been so idiotic about the whole
thing.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.269
He was being a real jerk.

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:50.580
And she was raging.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.959
We were both raging, we were on fire.

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:58.430
And then she turns to me and says: 'Wouldn't
it be great if we could have one free one

00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.430
a year?'

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.000
I knew exactly what she meant.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.829
And I said, 'Wow, honey.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:05.230
That's a bold statement!'

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.000
But it stayed with me."

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Close call

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.829
Scenestealer Rhys Wakefield was cast in The
Purge just one day before shooting began.

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:21.270
His character, known as the Polite Leader,
was arguably one of the most visually arresting

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:27.190
aspects of the movie, but it was a happy accident
that the right guy got the role.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:32.850
The Australian actor was chosen for the part
during an all-night casting call held out

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:38.870
of desperation the night before production
launched, and luckily, Wakefield's sly confidence

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:45.010
was just the right level of creepy to match
the role of leading a group that was inspired

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:49.890
by DeMonaco's fascination with the Manson
family murderers.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.830
Wedded bliss

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:56.030
Shane and Liz from The Purge: Anarchy are
actually married in real life.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:02.100
The actors, Zach Gilford and Kiele Sanchez,
auditioned to star as the on-screen couple

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.250
and boasted an authentic built-in connection
based on their real-life relationship.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:13.160
They'd been together since 2010, when they
met on the set of a failed TV pilot, and had

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:19.370
been together for four years before sharing
the screen again in the second Purge flick.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:20.690
Trump dumps

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:26.560
The third installment to the franchise, 2016's
The Purge: Election Year took a different

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:32.660
approach, keeping the traditional horror elements
but blending in some political thriller aspects.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:39.210
According to DeMonaco, the plot was inspired
by the 2016 race for the White House—if

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:42.900
not directly then certainly subconsciously.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.660
DeMonaco admitted that "drips" of what was
happening in the debates made it through to

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:54.190
him as he was penning the screenplay, though
he refused to say which characters represent

00:05:54.190 --> 00:06:00.340
which real-life politicians...in some cases,
it was pretty obvious.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.340
Next up

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:06.720
The Purge 3 was originally expected to arrive
as a prequel and go back to the night of the

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.930
first purge to explain the origins of the
New Founding Fathers, and now that a fourth

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:17.560
film is underway, that's the direction the
next installment may take.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.370
As DeMonaco told Cinema Blend:

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:24.100
"I would actually go back and I would try
to talk about how it all started.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:30.420
That seems the natural next chapter in this,
is to say, 'All right, we've done this trilogy.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:35.900
We see how it got to this point of someone
trying to end it, this presidential figure.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.060
Now let's go back and see how the f— all
this came about.'

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:45.750
How did this country get to a place where
we were now accepting this kind of atrocity,

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.950
this terrible thing, each year?

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.250
I think there's something interesting in that."

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:56.720
There's also a Purge TV show on the way, which
will showcase the gore and grit of different

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:03.420
purge night experiences and what drives ordinary
people to get to the point of participating

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:10.180
in such murder and mayhem in the first place
… you know aside from those ridiculous commercials.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.970
"I purge because staying in is unamerican."

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.680
"Don't let your freedoms get taken away."

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.150
Hey, maybe it'll be a good cautionary tale
for current times …

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:20.150
Thanks for watching!

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.470
